## ABSTRACT
In this paper, a dual-ligand design strategy is demonstrated to modulate the performance of the electronically conductive metal– organic frameworks (EC-MOFs) thin film with a spray layer-by-layer assembly method. The thin film not only can be precisely prepared in nanometer scale $(20{-}70\ \mathsf{n m})$ , but also shows the pin-hole-free smooth surface. The high quality nano-film of 2,3,6,7,10,11–hexaiminotriphenylene (HITP) doped Cu-HHTP enables the precise modulation of the chemiresistive sensitivity and selectivity. Selectivity improvement over $220\%$ were realized for benzene vs. $N H_{3}$ , as well as enhanced response and recovery properties. In addition, the selectivity of the EC-MOF thin film sensors toward other gases (e.g. triethylamine, methane, ethylbenzene, hydrogen, butanone, and acetone) vs. $N H_{3}$ at room temperature is also discussed.
## KEYWORDS
metal–organic frameworks, electronic conductivity, dual–ligand, thin films, gas sensors
## Introduction
Electronically conductive metal–organic frameworks (EC-MOFs) are a newly emerging type of porous conductive material constructed by the periodic connection of metal ions and redox-active organic units [1–5]. EC-MOFs possess crystalline state, narrowly distributed size and regularly arranged pores, the tunable band gap, as well as the designable charge transport pathway [6–14]. The unique combination of porosity and conductivity enables them to answer the question whether EC-MOFs can be applied to electrical applications. Together with the boosting researches on doped EC-MOFs or EC-MOF based composites, EC-MOFs as promising materials for electrical applications have been proofed by excellent cases including electrocatalysis [15], supercapacitors [16–18], chemiresistive gas sensors [3, 19–23], field effect transistors (FETs) [24], photo-catalysis [25, 26], and lithium-sulfur battery [27]. While semiconducting two-dimensional (2D) $\pi$ -conjugated EC-MOFs showed exciting properties as active materials with satisfactory stability [3, 6, 28], state of the art 2D $\pi$ -conjugated EC-MOF semiconductors mainly focus on single redox-active ligand systems with mono-/bi-metallic nodes [3, 28, 29]. Meanwhile, the effects of the dual-ligand with dissimilar functional motifs on the semiconducting behaviors of the 2D $\pi$ -conjugated ECMOF thin film have not been reported yet.
Considering the inherent simpleness in concept (resistance change), chemiresistive gas sensors are the simplest and most widely applied semiconductor devices among various gas sensors [30–35]. In terms of long-term bottlenecks of poor selectivity and high operating temperature, EC-MOFs are utilized as active sensing materials in consideration of their high porosity, specific guest-framework interaction and room temperature (RT) activity [13, 20, 31, 36–38], which can potentially cope with the issue. However, the detecting gases of the pristine EC-MOF chemiresistor are limited to strong reducing/oxidizing molecules such as $\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ , ${\mathrm{NH}}_{3},$ , $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{\mathrm{:}}$ , and NO, which hinders the extensive applications of such new generations of RT gas sensors. To broaden window of detectable gases by either improvements on quality of the film or introduction of a secondary MOF capable of gas separation [39, 40], both sensitivity and selectivity were enhanced by the fabrication of EC-MOF nano-film [20], and MOF-on-MOF thin films [41–43], respectively. Even so, continuous modulation of both sensitivity and selectivity by a single type of EC-MOF chemiresistor is still challenging.
Here, we propose a novel dual-ligand design strategy to modulate the performance of EC-MOF thin film chemiresistor by a spray layer-by-layer (LbL) liquid-phase epitaxial growth method. In this regard, two redox-active ligands with different coordination groups, namely 2,3,6,7,10,11-hexahydrotriphenylene (HHTP, –OH) and 2,3,6,7,10,11-hexaiminotriphenylene (HITP, $\mathrm{-NH}_{2}\mathrm{,}$ ), were utilized to prepare the hexagonal HITP doped Cu-HHTP-10C nanofilm (Fig. 1). The similar crystal structure of their pristine frameworks $\mathrm{(Cu_{3}(H H T P)_{2}}$ and $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}\mathrm{,}$ enabled the successful synthesis of dual-ligand EC-MOFs. Since the hybrid framework significantly altered the electronic structure, the chemiresistive sensing performances are thus modulated. The thin film not only can be precisely prepared in nanometer scale $(20-70~\mathrm{nm})$ , but also shows the pin-hole-free smooth surface. By this manner, the sensitivity and selectivity of the HITP doped Cu-HHTP-10C nanofilm toward different reducing gases can be well controlled. Moreover, enhanced sensing response and recovery properties were also realized by proper doping of HITP into $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTP})_{2}$ frameworks.
## 2 Results and discussion
Figure 2(a) and Figs. S1–S5 in the ESM show the atomic force microscopy (AFM) images of the typical top and edge view of the HITP doped Cu-HHTP-10C nano films. All the thin films are pinhole-free, dense and continuous, which can also be confirmed by scanning electron microscopy (SEM) measurements (Fig. S6 in the ESM). The HITP doping effects on the thickness and roughness of the thin film are summarized in Fig. 2(b). Compared with pristine $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTP})_{2}\mathrm{-}$ 10C thin film, the thickness gradually increased with increasing HITP dopants, while the calculated root mean square (RMS) surface roughness shows slight change for low HITP dopants but increases sharply when the dopant is higher than $5.0\mathrm{mol\%}$ . As mentioned above, the mixed ethanolic solution of dual ligands contains ammonium hydroxide used for the $\mathrm{\pH}$ value regulation of HITP. In our previous work, the thickness increments of Cu-HITP and Cu-HHTP are similar to each other ( $\sim2\:\mathrm{nm}$ per cycle). In contrast, the AFM results clearly reveal the enhanced growth of dual-ligand $\mathrm{cu}$ -HHTP/HITP thin film by ammonium hydroxide, especially in high concentration cases. The possible reason might lie in the remarkable growth of Cu-HHTP owing to $\mathrm{\pH}$ change of dual-ligand solutions. Even so, the thickness and roughness of all the HITP doped Cu-HHTP-10C nano films can be controlled to less than 80 and $15\mathrm{nm}$ , respectively. The dual ligand doping process can be further confirmed by monitoring HITP doped $\mathrm{Cu}$ -HHTP-10C nano films on the quartz substrate with the UV–Vis spectrum. As shown in Fig. S7 in the ESM, the intensity increments of the maximum absorption peaks for Cu-HITP $(212\mathrm{nm})$ and $\mathrm{Cu}$ -HHTP $353\mathrm{nm})$ differ from each other. Accordingly, the value of $I_{212\mathrm{nm}}/I_{353\mathrm{nm}}$ for the $5.0\ \mathrm{\mol}\%$ sample is higher than the $0.5\mathrm{\mol}\%$ sample because of the contribution of higher HITP dopants.
For the sample with high resistance $(>1,000\Omega)$ , the two-probe method is normally adopted for electrical measurements. For HITP dopants lower than $1.0\mathrm{mol}\%$ , the value of conductivity (defined as $\sigma/C_{0}$ here, details see the ESM) is notably increased $\sim2$ orders of magnitude due to the formation of highly conductive coordination of $\mathrm{Cu}$ ions with o-phenylenediamine linkages via the successful doping (Fig. 2(c)) [13, 21]. Nonetheless, for HITP dopants higher than $1.0\mathrm{\mol}\%$ , the $\sigma/C_{0}$ showed slight change due to negative effects of the reduced quality of thin films. The success doping of HITP can be further confirmed by X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) spectra with typical peaks of Cu, C, O and $\mathrm{~N~}$ (Fig. 2(d)). As a surface-sensitive semi-quantitative spectroscopic technique, high resolution O1s and N1s peaks indicate that the ratio of HITP to HHTP of the thin films increased with the increasing molar ratio of HITP and HHTP in the mixed dual-ligand solution (Fig. 2(e)). For the thin film using high molar ratio of HITP $(1.0\mathrm{\mol\%}$ or higher), significant peaks of non-lattice oxygen (vacancy $\mathrm{O}_{\mathrm{v}},$ chemisorbed or dissociated OH species $\mathrm{Oo}_{\mathrm{OH}}\rangle$ were observed. This may possibly owe to the competitive coordination of two different ligands with Cu ions, which gives rise to framework defects like missing Cu nodes, free –OH groups of HHTP, etc.
As active materials of chemiresistive gas sensors reported in recent years, pristine $\mathrm{Cu}$ -HHTP is a p-type semiconductive MOF material with a conductivity of $\sim0.02\ \mathrm{S{·}}\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ at RT (pellet, 2 probes) [44], while pristine Cu-HITP is a $\mathtt{n-/p}$ -type (depends on defects formed during synthesis) semiconductive MOF material with a conductivity of $\sim0.2\ \mathrm{S{{·}}\mathrm{cm^{-1}}}$ at RT (pellet, 2 probes) [21]. The combination of two ligands with different redox activity enables the modulation of electronic structure due to differences in the contribution to highest occupied molecular orbital (HOMO) levels, defect-related energy levels, etc. Therefore, the activated HITP doped Cu-HHTP-10C nano films on sapphire substrates precoated with interdigital Au electrodes were directly used as the chemiresistors for the detection of 8 different reducing gases (for details, see the ESM).
Figure 3(a) shows the typical response–recovery curve of HITP doped Cu-HHTP-10C to benzene gas of different concentrations $\left(1{-}100\mathrm{ppm}\right)$ . All sensors exhibited the increased resistance upon exposure toward benzene gas, which is the typical behaviour of a $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{\tt~p~}}$ -type semiconductor and consistent with the pristine Cu-HHTP. Interestingly, the normalized current curves present good response–recovery to $100\mathrm{ppm}$ of benzene gas (Fig. 3(b)). Although the thicker film always brings about poor response and recovery properties due to the longer gas diffusion path, the existence of HITP in the framework shows more profoundly accelerating effects than thickness. Experimentally, the response time $\left(t_{\mathrm{res}}\right)$ and recovery time $\left(t_{\mathrm{rec}}\right)$ are significantly reduced to $<1$ min and $<10~\mathrm{min}$ , respectively, when the doped HITP is $1.0\mathrm{\mol}\%$ or higher, which is satisfied compared with the reported chemiresistors working at room/low temperature [5, 20–23, 45, 46]. Response– concentration log–log plots show good linearity for all sensors, based on which the detection limit of each sensor can be deduced to be $0.024-0.096\mathrm{ppm}$ (trace-level) by setting response $=10\%$ (Fig. 3(c)) [35, 47, 48].
Figure 3(d) shows the responses of $x\mathrm{~mol\%~}$ HITP doped Cu-HHTP-10C $\stackrel{\cdot}{x}=0.1$ , 0.5, 1.0, 5.0, 10.0) toward $100~\mathrm{ppm}$ $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ and benzene (3 sensors for each molar ratio), respectively. Both a remarkably decreased response to $\mathrm{NH}_{3},$ , from $\sim240\%$ to $\sim80\%$ , and a narrowly changed response to benzene between $75\%$ and $90\%$ can be observed. The selectivity $(S=R_{\mathrm{benzene}}/R_{\mathrm{NH3}})$ of HITP doped Cu-HHTP-10C towards benzene and $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ was gradually improved by increased doping amounts of HITP ligands into $\mathrm{Cu}$ -HHTP frameworks. An improvement in S up to \~ $220\%$ was obtained by $10.0\mathrm{\mol}\%$ HITP doped CuHHTP-10C, of which the sensitivity toward $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ and benzene is reversed.
To further investigate the modulated sensitivity and selectivity of HITP doped Cu-HHTP-10C, they were studied in discriminating amongst eight typical human breath biomarkers and common interfering gases $(\mathrm{NH}_{3},$ , triethylamine (TEA, $\Nu(\mathrm{Et})_{3})$ , methane, benzene, ethylbenzene, hydrogen, butanone, and acetone). The responses of $x\mod\%$ HITP doped Cu-HHTP-10C $(x=0.1,0.5,1.0,5.0,10.0)$ to these gases are summarized in Fig. 3(e). Similar to $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ , HITP doping merely results in poor sensing responses toward hydrogen, butanone, ethylbenzene and acetone. Conversely, enhanced responses were observed for alkanes and TEA. As for selectivity of gases vs. $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ $(S=R_{\mathrm{gas}}/R_{\mathrm{NH3}})$ , while there are improvements for all gases with sensors of $1.0\mathrm{\mol}\%$ HITP dopants or higher, both reduction and enhancement can be found for those with low HITP dopants, which can be attributed to the competitive sensing activity contribution of two different coordinated metal-ligands (Fig. 3(f)). Notably, for TEA, a molecule constructed by replacing three $^{-\mathrm{H}}$ of $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ with $-\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{CH}_{3}$ , all sensors $\mathrm{0.1\mol\%-10.0\mol\%}$ HITP) show enhanced selectivity vs. $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ . The highest improvement in S of up to $\sim227\%$ (10.0 $1\mathrm{mol}\%$ HITP) is similar to benzene gas and far better than other gases $(\sim100\%$ S improvement), which may be the result of the doping induced differences on the gaseous analyte-sensing material interactions and the efficiency of charge transfer.
It is difficult to reveal the exact mechanism for the sensing performances of Cu-HHTP/HITP, HITP doped Cu-HHTP or other EC-MOFs. Experimentally, ultraviolet photoelectron spectroscopy (UPS) measurements of the EC-MOFs thin film show the up-shift of Fermi level (n-type doping effects) with the increment of HITP dopants (Figs. 4(a) and 4(b)). Despite of the shifts of Fermi level, all thin films on Au electrodes (work function, $\sim5.1\ \mathrm{eV},$ ) showed good ohmic contacts (Fig. S8 in the ESM). From the simple viewpoints of charge transfer and the resistance baseline, the $\mathfrak{n}$ -type doping effects and reduced resistance are in fact not good. For instance, there is lower sensitivity to smaller charge transfers upon exposure to low concentration reducing gas adsorption, which explains the reason for continuously reduced responses of sensors toward $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ with increased HITP dopants. Interestingly, the rich defects in thin films with HITP dopants of $1.0\ \mathrm{\mol}\%-10.0\ \mathrm{\mol}\%$ might be conducive to charge transfer of the framework with benzene and TEA, resulting in good sensing responses and consequently selectivity improvement over $220\%$ .
## 3 Conclusions
In summary, a new dual-ligand design strategy to modulate the performance of the EC-MOF thin film was reported for the RT chemiresistive gas sensor. With a spray LbL assembly method, two redox-active ligands with different coordination groups were utilized to prepare the hexagonal HITP doped Cu-HHTP-10C nanofilm. The thin film not only can be precisely prepared in nanometer scale $\left(20\mathrm{-}70\ \mathrm{nm}\right)$ , but also shows pin hole free smooth surface. Besides, the sensitivity and selectivity of the HITP doped Cu-HHTP-10C nanofilm toward $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ and benzene were found to be well controlled. Selectivity improvement over $220\%$ was realized for benzene vs. $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ due to rich defects caused by HITP doping. Moreover, enhanced sensing response and recovery properties were also realized by proper doping of HITP. These results indicate the potential application of the RT MOF sensor for highly sensitive and selective real-time monitoring and timely alarming of a broad range of toxic, explosive or flammable gases.
